      "title": "Fractional CMO Economics: Positioning, Pricing & Value Architecture",
      "module": "The CMO Mandate & Strategic Framework",
      "estimatedMinutes": 30,
      "content": "<h2>Fractional CMO Is a Business, Not a Consulting Arrangement</h2><p>The fractional CMO market has grown dramatically as companies have recognized that they need CMO-level strategic leadership but cannot justify or afford a full-time $250-400K executive at their current stage. This market growth has also created competitive pressure: the number of people calling themselves fractional CMOs has grown faster than the market's ability to distinguish between genuinely experienced executive leaders and marketing managers who have adopted a label. The fractional CMO who wants to command premium rates and build a practice rather than a gig portfolio must make deliberate choices about positioning, pricing, engagement structure, and client selection.</p><p>The most important mindset shift for a new fractional CMO is from \"hired consultant\" to \"practice owner.\" A consultant is engaged to do work; a practice owner builds an asset. The practice owner approach means: defining a specific market position (what type of company do you serve, at what stage, with what specialty), pricing for sustainable business economics rather than hourly opportunity, structuring engagements for long-term relationships rather than project completion, and investing in brand-building activities (content, speaking, community) that generate inbound opportunities rather than depending on outbound networking.</p><h2>Fractional CMO Positioning</h2><p>Positioning specificity is the single most important practice-building decision. The general \"fractional CMO for B2B companies\" positioning competes with everyone. The specific \"fractional CMO for B2B SaaS companies at Series A and B raising their first real marketing function\" or \"fractional CMO for PE-backed portfolio companies navigating growth acceleration\" compete with a much smaller, more identifiable set of practitioners, command higher rates from clients who see their specific expertise as a premium, and generate referrals because clients can describe the niche clearly when recommending to colleagues.</p><p>Positioning is validated by the engagements you've done, the clients who will serve as references, and the content you produce that demonstrates your specific expertise publicly. A fractional CMO whose website and LinkedIn demonstrate deep SaaS demand generation expertise, whose references include three Series B CMO alumni who found success, and who publishes thought leadership about SaaS pipeline metrics is positioned. One whose website says \"helping companies grow through strategic marketing\" is not.</p><h2>Pricing Architecture</h2><p>Fractional CMO pricing has three common structures. Monthly retainer: a fixed fee for a defined scope of hours and deliverables per month, typically ranging from $5,000 per month for 2-4 hours per week at smaller companies to $20,000+ per month for 15-20 hours per week at growth-stage companies with significant marketing leadership needs. Project-based: a fixed fee for a defined deliverable (go-to-market strategy, marketing audit, positioning overhaul), typically ranging from $15,000 to $75,000 depending on scope and timeline. Equity + cash hybrid: a reduced cash fee combined with a small equity stake (typically 0.1-0.5%), appropriate when the company is cash-constrained but has compelling growth potential.</p><p>The most common pricing error is underpricing, which occurs for two reasons: comparison to employee hourly rates rather than professional services market rates, and failure to price the full value delivered (strategic direction, avoided hiring mistakes, accelerated execution) rather than just the hours worked. A fractional CMO who develops a positioning strategy that positions the company to close a $2M enterprise deal is not delivering $3,000 of value in the hours they spent - they're delivering a leveraged return on a strategic investment. Pricing should reflect economic value, not time.</p><h2>Engagement Structure for Long-Term Success</h2><p>Engagement structure determines whether fractional CMO work is sustainable. Three-month initial contracts with renewal options are the appropriate starting structure: long enough to demonstrate value, short enough that both parties can exit gracefully if the fit isn't right. Monthly reporting to the CEO or COO that demonstrates specific commercial outcomes keeps the engagement visible and defensible. Quarterly value reviews - explicit conversations about whether the engagement is meeting both parties' expectations - prevent drift and enable recalibration before small misalignments become contract-ending frustrations.</p>",
