      "content": "<h2>Go-to-Market Strategy as a Repeatable Discipline</h2><p>Go-to-market (GTM) strategy - the coordinated plan for how a company will reach and acquire customers in a specific market - is not a one-time exercise completed at company founding. Every new product, every new customer segment, and every new geographic market requires a GTM strategy that is specific to that opportunity's economics, competitive dynamics, and buying behavior. Companies that apply their original GTM strategy to new segments without adaptation are making the mistake of assuming that what worked in the first market will work in every market - an assumption that fails more often than it succeeds.</p><p>The CMO who owns GTM strategy brings the market perspective that product teams often lack: deep understanding of how the target segment actually buys, which channels reach them effectively, what messages resonate in that segment specifically, and which competitive alternatives they're comparing. This market knowledge is the input that converts a product capability into a viable commercial offering. Products that have strong underlying capabilities but weak GTM strategies generate disappointing initial traction - not because the product is wrong, but because the go-to-market didn't reflect how the target segment actually makes purchasing decisions.</p><h2>The GTM Framework</h2><p>A complete GTM strategy covers six elements. Market definition: who exactly is this for, with what firmographic and behavioral characteristics that make them a high-fit prospect? The market definition must be specific enough to build a target account list and broad enough to support the revenue target. Problem definition: what specific problem does this offer solve, and how does the target segment currently solve it (whether through a competitor product or a homegrown workaround)? Channel strategy: how will the offer reach the target segment - through what sales motion (inbound, outbound, partner-led, product-led), what marketing channels, and what distribution relationships? Pricing and packaging: at what price point and in what package configuration will the offer be positioned for the target segment, and how does that compare to the economics of the alternatives they're currently using?</p><p>Success metrics and milestones: what are the specific indicators that the GTM is working - and at what revenue, pipeline, and engagement thresholds will the company decide to accelerate, adjust, or discontinue the strategy? This milestone structure is the most neglected element of most GTM plans. Without explicit decision points, companies continue investing in GTM strategies that are underperforming past the point where the evidence has clearly indicated that adjustment is needed. Explicit milestones convert GTM strategy from a commitment to an investment with defined exit criteria.</p><h2>Channel Strategy: Direct, Partner, and Product-Led</h2><p>Three primary GTM channel strategies apply to different market situations. Direct sales (outbound or inbound-fed) is most appropriate for high-ACV, complex sales with multiple stakeholders - the investment in direct sales capacity is justified by the deal size. Partner-led distribution is most appropriate for markets where partners have established trust relationships with the target buyers and where the company's brand doesn't yet have the recognition to sell independently. Product-led growth (PLG) is most appropriate for products with strong bottom-up adoption potential - where individual users can discover, adopt, and advocate for the product within their organizations without requiring a sales conversation.</p><p>Hybrid GTM motions - combining PLG with enterprise sales, for example - are increasingly common but require careful design: the product-led motion creates awareness and usage within target accounts, and the sales motion converts product-led usage into enterprise contracts. The PLG + enterprise hybrid works when there is a natural upgrade path from self-serve to enterprise, and when the sales team is configured to identify and qualify accounts based on product usage signals rather than inbound inquiry.</p>",
