      "title": "Revenue Operations: Marketing's Role in the RevOps Function",
      "module": "Growth Marketing & Scale",
      "estimatedMinutes": 30,
      "content": "<h2>Revenue Operations Is the Commercial Infrastructure the CMO Relies On</h2><p>Revenue Operations (RevOps) - the function that aligns sales, marketing, and customer success around shared data, processes, and technology to improve commercial efficiency - has emerged as one of the most important organizational innovations in B2B go-to-market design. For CMOs, the RevOps function is either an enabling infrastructure that makes marketing programs measurable, attributable, and optimizable, or a fragmented set of disconnected systems that make it impossible to answer basic questions about marketing's commercial contribution. The CMO who invests in a strong marketing-RevOps partnership builds the foundation for data-driven marketing; the one who allows RevOps to become a siloed operations function creates the measurement problems that undermine marketing credibility in commercial conversations.</p><p>Marketing's specific dependencies on RevOps infrastructure are concrete and commercially important. Attribution: accurately connecting marketing activities to pipeline and revenue requires CRM data quality and integration architecture that RevOps owns. Lead routing: the process that routes marketing-generated leads to the right sales rep at the right time with the right context requires RevOps configuration and maintenance. Campaign tracking: accurate tracking of campaign performance in the CRM requires RevOps-maintained UTM conventions, lead source fields, and campaign attribution standards. Forecasting: marketing's contribution to the company pipeline forecast requires RevOps reporting infrastructure that produces reliable attribution data. Without RevOps partnership, all of these are at risk - and when they fail, marketing loses the measurement capability that defends its budget and guides its investment decisions.</p><h2>The Marketing-RevOps Operating Model</h2><p>The most effective marketing-RevOps partnership model has marketing bringing the commercial requirements - what outcomes need to be measured, what campaign processes need to be tracked, what attribution logic should govern pipeline credit - and RevOps bringing the technical implementation capability that makes those requirements functional in the CRM and analytics stack. Conflicts arise when marketing makes technology decisions without RevOps involvement (creating systems that don't integrate with the existing stack) or when RevOps makes process decisions without marketing input (creating tracking conventions that don't capture the data marketing needs to defend its programs). The solution is a joint marketing-RevOps planning process at the start of each quarter that aligns on priorities, resolves resource conflicts, and documents the specific implementations that each function needs from the other.</p><h2>The CMO's RevOps Agenda</h2><p>The specific RevOps investments that most benefit marketing are: pipeline attribution accuracy (getting first-touch and multi-touch attribution working correctly in the CRM so marketing can report on sourced pipeline with confidence), marketing-qualified lead routing (ensuring MQLs are routed to the right rep within the SLA timeframe and that follow-up compliance is tracked), campaign performance reporting (a standard reporting framework that connects campaign investment to pipeline and revenue outcomes in a consistent, comparable format across all programs), and contact data quality (maintaining ICP-fit contact data that makes segmentation and personalization effective).</p>",
